A||||5||btoth||Spark plug gap on V6||||4||
Z||000000||btoth||06-09-2001||01:11 PM||||Well, some of you may recall that I was having some problems with my new 2.8... idle and running rough sometimes.  Anyway, I replaced the coil with one from MSD and today I decided to check my spark plugs to see exactly what they used in the new engine after installing it.  Well, they're Autolite brand (I was hoping to see AC Delco in there)... and besides that, they weren't even gaped right!!!  They were all gaped at about 0.038-0.040.  Well, I gaped #1,3,4 and 5 to 0.045 like it says under the deck lid but #2 and #6 are in there soooooo tight that I can't possibly take them out myself.  I guess I'll have to find someone big to help with the last two now that I've gapped the others... but what I'd like to know is, what exactly are symptoms of incorrectly gaped spark plugs, and can -0.005 make that much of a difference in how the engine runs???  I think it's really stupid that they couldn't even gap the plugs correctly when it specifically says right on the underside of the deck lid. *sigh*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>On a side note.... I noticed a bunch of crap from the trees over my driveway stuck in the recessed area that the spark plugs go into... I can't get a vacuum back there and I tried cleaning them out with a wet paper towel (these are the plugs I didn't remove anyway)... but would it be really bad if a couple dried leaves were to fall down inside???<P>Sorry so long... thanks for reading.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Mach10||06-09-2001||04:24 PM||||Yes, bits of crud and dried leaves would be bad for the engine. What I do is get  small flathead, and wrap part of an old rag in it, and tape it down. Douse the area in solvent, and "chisel" the crap from around the plug, and wipe it away. Works well, in my experience.||||reg||7
Z||000002||StuGood||06-09-2001||10:28 PM||||Under certain conditions, I suppose mis-gapped plugs could cause detectable problems.<P>To get stubborn plugs out, I use a flex head ratchet and a cheater bar, plus liberal amounts of Liquid Wrench, care and patience.  I'd give myself a weekend free to get the uncooperative plugs out - in the meantime, it probably won't matter much if you mix brands.  <BR> <BR>FWIW, I believe theogre mentioned in an earlier post that he uses Autolite plugs in his Fiero, and is perfectly happy with those.  I have always been happy with AC plugs & wires.  No brand name wars, please  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I sympatize with your desire to get all the same type plug and gap.  <P>One of the biggest benefits I get from changing plugs, is that I can examine the deposits on them, if any. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||06-10-2001||12:15 PM||||Well, one of my friends (much bigger than me) came over and got the stubborn plugs out.  Good thing too, they were gapped even worse than the others... close to 0.038" on the one.  I have no problem with the type of plugs really, if they work they work.  I do wish they would have gapped them properly though. I have noticed much better starting and better idle... and no lag in acceleration (except when the engine is cold) now that I've gapped the plugs properly.  The reason the plugs were stuck was because they were really rusted.  I hate how water and other junk collects on the front side of the engine like it does.<BR> <BR>Anyone know of any plugs that don't rust up like that?  (Just for future knowledge.)  I've only had these in there for two weeks!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||06-10-2001||12:27 PM||||Anyone who owns a Fiero that sees a lot of weather should get in the habit of replacing the plugs more often than you would on any other car. I've seen plugs that had almost become a permanent part of the car because the nut on the plug was almost completely rusted away!!<BR>Anti-seize isn't a bad idea either.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000005||kslish||06-11-2001||03:39 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>Anyone know of any plugs that don't rust up like that?  (Just for future knowledge.)  I've only had these in there for two weeks!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Spend a little extra and get a premium plug with a coated housing.  This is the main reason I use AC/Delco Rapidfires....the housings are nickel coated so they don't rust in place like the plain steel plugs do.  The extra cost is worth it to me the next time I go to change the plugs.<P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
